  "thumbnailAlt": "Therapy Intake form beside a photo of a therapist and a client engaging in a counseling session in a bright, modern office",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A therapy intake form is a form that collects a new client's contact details, reasons for seeking support, current symptoms, past mental health treatment, safety concerns and goals so a therapist can prepare for the first session and confirm the client is a good fit. This template uses plain, non-judgmental wording, keeps sensitive questions optional where it can, and ends with an informed consent statement covering confidentiality and its limits.",
    "audience": "Licensed therapists, counselors, psychologists, social workers in private practice, group practices and campus or employee counseling services.",
    "useCases": [
      "Sending paperwork to a new client after the free consultation call",
      "Screening referrals for a group practice before matching to a clinician",
      "Intake for telehealth clients who never visit the office",
      "University counseling centers handling student sign-ups at the start of term"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Require sign-in and one response per person so each client completes intake once and can save and come back later.",
      "Turn on email notifications so you see answers to the safety questions as soon as a form arrives, not on the day of the session.",
      "Use a PDF template to turn each completed intake into a tidy file for the client's record."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a therapy intake form include?",
        "answer": "Contact and emergency contact details, the main reason the client is seeking therapy, current symptoms and how long they have lasted, past therapy or psychiatric treatment, current medications, a safety question about thoughts of self-harm, substance use, goals for therapy, session preferences, and informed consent covering confidentiality and its limits."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I ask about suicidal thoughts on an intake form?",
        "answer": "Many clinicians include a short safety question so they can follow up at the first session, but a form is not a crisis service. Tell clients clearly who to contact in an emergency, and review safety answers promptly; this template includes that wording."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is an online therapy intake form private?",
        "answer": "Responses are kept in your private account and are not shown publicly. Rules for mental health records vary by country and state, so check your local health-privacy and licensing requirements before you collect client information online."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long should a therapy intake form be?",
        "answer": "Long enough to prepare for the first session, short enough to finish in about 10 to 15 minutes. Leave detailed history for the session itself and mark deeper questions as optional."
      }
    ]
  },
